2. Theoretical Foundations: From Mechanism to Scale

2.1 The Mechanism Taxonomy

The literature establishes several mechanisms of cooperation:

\text{Kinship} \rightarrow \text{Direct reciprocity} \rightarrow \text{Indirect reciprocity} \rightarrow \text{Reputation} \rightarrow \text{Punishment} \rightarrow \text{Institutions}

Axelrod & Hamilton (1981) provide the classical repeated-interaction foundation. Nowak (2006) formalizes several mechanisms including direct reciprocity, indirect reciprocity, network reciprocity, and group selection. Henrich & Muthukrishna (2021) emphasize that human cooperation involves genetic and cultural evolution, with mechanisms including kinship, reciprocity, reputation, signaling, punishment, norms, and institutions.

However, these mechanisms do not all scale equally. The central theoretical insight is that different cooperation mechanisms have different scaling properties, and this has not been systematically formalized.

2.2 The Scaling Constraint: Powers & Lehmann (2017)

Powers & Lehmann (2017) provide the critical scaling constraint. They demonstrate that:

· As group size increases, information about past behavior becomes harder to transmit, reducing the effectiveness of reciprocity.
· Relatedness between actor and recipient decreases rapidly with increasing group size.
· Reciprocity—whether direct, indirect, or generalized—cannot sustain cooperation in very large groups under realistic conditions of information transmission.
· Large-scale cooperation in human societies therefore requires institutional mechanisms.

This yields the central theoretical chain:

\boxed{
\text{Group size} \rightarrow \text{information constraints} \rightarrow \text{reciprocity limits} \rightarrow \text{mechanism substitution} \rightarrow \text{institutional cooperation}
}

The MCH emerges from this literature rather than appearing as an entirely new theory.

2.3 Mechanism-Specific Scaling Limits

Define:

M_j(n) = \text{effectiveness of cooperation mechanism } j \text{ at scale } n

Each mechanism has a characteristic scale n_j^* at which it becomes insufficient:

n_j^* = \text{characteristic scale at which mechanism } j \text{ becomes insufficient}

The central scientific contribution is the study of mechanism-specific scaling limits.

Proposition 1 (Mechanism Scaling). Different cooperation mechanisms have different scaling limits. When the interaction scale exceeds the effective operating range of a mechanism, cooperation requires substitution, augmentation, or institutionalization of that mechanism.

2.4 Functional Equivalence

This concept is central to the thesis.

\boxed{
\text{Functional equivalence} = \text{different mechanisms performing the same cooperation-preserving function}
}

The same cooperation function can be implemented by completely different social mechanisms:

Function Small-scale mechanism Large-scale mechanism
Trust Personal knowledge Institutional reputation
Monitoring Observation Bureaucracy/data systems
Sanction Interpersonal punishment Courts/state enforcement
Information Conversation Records/media/information networks
Reputation Gossip Formal reputation systems
Coordination Shared norms Formal rules
Conflict resolution Elders/mediation Courts/arbitration
Reciprocity Direct exchange Generalized institutions

The central question becomes:

How are cooperation-preserving functions maintained when the mechanisms that originally performed them cease to scale?

