3. The Multiscale Cooperation Hypothesis (MCH)

3.1 Formal Statement

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Human cooperation is maintained by multiple mechanisms whose} \\
&\text{effectiveness depends on the scale and structure of interaction.} \\
&\text{As social scale increases, mechanisms based on direct knowledge,} \\
&\text{repeated interaction, and interpersonal monitoring may encounter} \\
&\text{informational, demographic, and network constraints.} \\
&\text{Stable cooperation at larger scales therefore requires functional} \\
&\text{substitution, augmentation, or institutionalization of these mechanisms.} \\
&\text{The probability of coordination failure and intergroup conflict should} \\
&\text{increase when the effective scale of social interaction grows faster} \\
&\text{than the capacity of the mechanisms responsible for information,} \\
&\text{coordination, monitoring, legitimacy, enforcement, and dispute resolution.}
\end{aligned}
}

3.2 The Mathematical Core

The scale-transition operator:

\boxed{
\mathbf{X}_{t+1} = \mathcal{T}\left(\mathbf{X}_t, \mathbf{S}_t, \mathbf{I}_t, \mathbf{G}_t, \mathbf{E}_t\right)
}

where \mathcal{T} is the scale-transition operator.

The institutional scale mismatch:

\boxed{
\Delta S_I = S_{\text{interaction}} - S_{\text{institutional}}
}

where:

· S_{\text{interaction}} = effective complexity/scale of interactions
· S_{\text{institutional}} = capacity of institutions to process, monitor, and coordinate those interactions

The central prediction:

\boxed{
\Delta S_I > 0 \Rightarrow P(\text{coordination failure}) \uparrow
}

while:

\boxed{
\text{functional substitution} \Rightarrow \Delta S_I \downarrow \Rightarrow P(\text{cooperation}) \uparrow
}

3.3 Scale as a Vector

Scale is not a single scalar. It is a vector of interacting dimensions:

\mathbf{S} = (P, D_s, H, K, A_n, \tau)

where:

Symbol Definition
P Population size / number of agents
D_s Spatial and social distance
H Institutional hierarchy depth
K Cultural, linguistic, and economic heterogeneity
A_n Anonymity of interaction
\tau Temporal horizon and interaction frequency

Cooperation may scale differently depending on which dimension increases. A dense trade network of 10,000 strangers is not equivalent to a territorial state of 10,000 individuals. The model must specify which dimension of scale is being varied.

3.4 Multidimensional Institutional Capacity

We reject the treatment of institutions as a single scalar. Institutional capacity is multidimensional:

\boxed{
\mathbf{I} = (I_m, I_i, I_s, I_e, I_l, I_r)
}

where:

Symbol Component
I_m Monitoring capacity
I_i Information capacity
I_s Sanctioning capacity
I_e Enforcement capacity
I_l Legitimacy
I_r Dispute-resolution capacity

This is motivated by experimental evidence. Baldassarri & Grossman (2013) found that centralized sanctioning can promote cooperation and that legitimacy of the authority matters; their field experiment involved 1,543 Ugandan farmers in 50 producer cooperatives. Their experiment found greater responsiveness to an elected monitor than a randomly selected monitor, indicating an experimentally supported role for legitimacy in cooperation.

Therefore:

\mathbf{I} \succeq \mathbf{I}^*(\mathbf{S})

where institutional capacity is multidimensional and the threshold is a vector.

3.5 Institutional Scale Mismatch

Define:

\boxed{
\Delta S_I = S_{\text{social}} - S_{\text{institutional}}
}

where:

· S_{\text{social}} = effective complexity/scale of interactions
· S_{\text{institutional}} = capacity of institutions to process, monitor, and coordinate those interactions

Then:

\Delta S_I > 0 \Longleftrightarrow I < I^*(S)

Institutional mismatch becomes the empirical concept; I^* becomes one mathematical representation of it.

Proposition 2 (Institutional Scale Mismatch). When the effective scale of social interaction exceeds the scale at which institutions can perform cooperation-preserving functions, the probability of coordination failure increases.

Formally:

\frac{\partial P(\text{failure})}{\partial \Delta S_I} > 0

3.6 Institutional Complexity ≠ Institutional Effectiveness

A society can have:

I_{\text{complexity}} \gg 1

but:

I_{\text{effectiveness}} \approx 0

Conversely, relatively simple institutions may work extremely well under appropriate conditions. Therefore:

I = (I_c, I_e)

where:

· I_c = complexity
· I_e = effectiveness

The thesis tests I_e rather than assuming I_c \approx I_e.

3.7 Functional Equivalence and Substitution

\boxed{
\text{Functional equivalence} = \text{different mechanisms performing the same cooperation-preserving function}
}

The central question becomes:

How are cooperation-preserving functions maintained when the mechanisms that originally performed them cease to scale?

3.8 Minimum Sufficient Governance Scale

Define:

G^* = \min\{s : \text{institutional prerequisites are satisfied at scale } s\}

Then investigate whether problems governed at:

s < G^*

produce coordination failures, while forcing problems into:

s \gg G^*

creates unnecessary complexity or legitimacy problems.

