6. Evidence Architecture and Methodology

6.1 Triangulation Principle

No single discipline is treated as sufficient. The research triangulates:

\text{Archaeological} + \text{Ethnographic} + \text{Experimental} + \text{Historical} + \text{Network} + \text{Mathematical}

The central methodological rule:

Do not use a theoretical claim as evidence for itself. Every major model component must be connected to an empirical source.

6.2 Phase 1 — Systematic Review and Evidence Banks

The research constructs three distinct evidence banks:

Evidence Bank A — Mechanism Evidence. Contains T, R, G, \mathbf{I}, M, U, \ldots and asks: Does this mechanism actually affect cooperation?

Evidence Bank B — Scale Evidence. Contains P, D, H, K, A, \tau and asks: Does the effectiveness of the mechanism change as scale changes?

Evidence Bank C — Boundary Conditions & Contradictions. Contains:

Field Content
CLAIM Statement being evaluated
SUPPORTING_STUDIES Sources supporting
CONTRADICTING_STUDIES Sources contradicting
DATASET Data source
METHOD_DIFFERENCE Methodological divergence
POPULATION_DIFFERENCE Sample divergence
TIME_PERIOD Temporal scope
MEASUREMENT_DIFFERENCE Operationalization divergence
POSSIBLE_RECONCILIATION Synthesis attempt
CURRENT_STATUS Resolved/contested/unknown

This directly separates:

\boxed{\text{Does mechanism X work?}}

from:

\boxed{\text{Does mechanism X continue working when scale increases?}}

