Each claim is classified as:

\{\text{empirical}, \text{theoretical}, \text{model-derived}, \text{correlational}, \text{causal}, \text{contested}, \text{unknown}\}

No hypothesis is converted into a fact merely because multiple papers repeat it. Every claim is traced to a source, and every source is evaluated for quality, replication, and alternative interpretation.

6.4 Phase 2 — Formal Model and Agent-Based Simulation

An agent-based model is constructed with agents characterized by T_i, R_i, and A_{ij}. The network G is dynamic. Institutions \mathbf{I} emerge as rules when P > P^*.

Calibration uses independently estimated parameters:

Parameter Source Value / Constraint
w (shadow of the future) Axelrod 0.5
\beta_{T \times P} (trust-punishment interaction) Balliet & Van Lange \approx 0.31
Raid participation effect Glowacki et al. 19.2% per nonleader friend
F_{st} range Bowles [0.08, 0.15] (contested)
F_{st}^* (critical threshold) Dyble reanalysis Empirical F_{ST} \approx 0.012

Note on Glowacki effect size: The actual Nyangatom raiding-party study reports that a Nyangatom man was 19.2% more likely to participate in a raid for each nonleader friend also participating, versus 6.8% for a leader-friend.

Simulations run over 10^4 generations across three nested scales. Sensitivity analysis, calibration on independent data, and out-of-sample validation are mandatory.

Pseudo-code:

```
for n in scales:
    C[n+1] = T(C[n], T[n], R[n], I[n], G[n], S[n], E[n])
    if I[n] < I*(S[n]):
        W[n+1] = G(M[n], R_c[n], U[n], C_p[n], I[n])
```

6.5 Phase 3 — Empirical Validation

Three empirical tests are conducted:

1. Seshat validation. Test the distributional prediction that C_{n+1} declines when \mathbf{I} \prec \mathbf{I}^* even if T and R are high. Test scaling laws against Seshat data with explicit attention to missing-data treatment.
2. Archaeological baseline. Use Keeley/Kelly and Fry/Söderberg data as competing baselines for W without \mathbf{I}. Quantify preservation bias and coding disagreement explicitly.
3. Civil war and raid case studies. Use Kalyvas and Glowacki data to test the prediction that U moderates the distribution of violence and that C_p predicts coalition formation.

6.6 Seshat Uncertainty as First-Class Model Variable

Seshat is not a perfect observational database. Its own methodology acknowledges substantial missing information; one major analysis reported approximately 3,700 missing values among 21,000 data points in the analyzed dataset. Methodological work emphasizes that social complexity itself is multidimensional and difficult to quantify.

Therefore:

Y^* = Y_{\text{observed}} + \epsilon_{\text{coding}} + \epsilon_{\text{missing}}

The empirical model explicitly distinguishes:

\text{historical reality}

from:

\text{coded historical observation}

At minimum, conduct: complete-case analysis; multiple imputation; worst/best-case bounds; sensitivity analysis; analysis restricted to high-attestation cases.

6.7 Archaeological Baseline as Robustness Module

Do not try to resolve the archaeological war debate inside the thesis. Make the disagreement itself part of the methodology.

Create:

W_{\text{arch}}^{(K)}

for the Keeley/Kelly-style interpretation and:

W_{\text{arch}}^{(F)}

for competing lower-violence estimates.

Then run:

\text{MCH}(W^{(K)}) \qquad \text{and} \qquad \text{MCH}(W^{(F)})

If the main conclusions survive both:

\boxed{\text{robust to archaeological baseline uncertainty}}

This is stronger scientifically than selecting one side.

6.8 Causal Identification Strategy

The variables C, \mathbf{I}, P, G, W are co-determined. The research addresses endogeneity through:

Design 1 — Exogenous demographic shock: The Black Death (1347–1351) reduced European populations by 30–50% without immediate institutional change. However, population decline can simultaneously change labor scarcity, wages, land values, elite power, state capacity, migration, and conflict incentives. Do not preregister the direction as automatically W \downarrow. Instead formulate competing predictions.

Design 2 — Information shock: The introduction of the telegraph and radio reduced U dramatically. Test: Does P(W \mid R_c, B) decrease? This is plausible but highly confounded by state capacity, military mobilization, and political transformation.

Design 3 — Network shock: The Bronze Age collapse destroyed trade-network bridges, reducing G_{\text{density}}. MCH prediction: \mathbf{I}^* increases, making cooperation harder to sustain. This is interesting but much too speculative for a primary causal identification strategy.

Recommendation: Move all three into:

\boxed{\text{Exploratory causal case studies}}

until the identification assumptions are independently demonstrated.

Additional strategies include dynamic panel models with lagged dependent variables and instrumental variables where valid instruments exist; process tracing for mechanism evidence; cultural phylogenetics to control for Galton's problem and shared ancestry; pre-registration of hypotheses and analyses for experimental components.

Causal language is used only when identification is credible. Otherwise, associations are reported as such.

6.9 Causal Graph Before Statistical Models

Construct a DAG before choosing statistical models:

E \rightarrow R_c

P \rightarrow \mathbf{I}

P \rightarrow U

G \rightarrow U

U \rightarrow B

\mathbf{I} \rightarrow C

\mathbf{I} \rightarrow W

R_c \rightarrow W

B \rightarrow W

and feedback:

W \rightarrow \mathbf{I}

W \rightarrow G

This reveals which variables are confounders, mediators, colliders, outcomes, feedback variables. Only after this should one decide between panel models, SEM, Bayesian models, survival models, IV, etc.

6.10 Bayesian Model Comparison

Given the uncertainty in historical data, Bayesian methods may be particularly appropriate. Compare:

M_1 = \text{direct trust model}

M_2 = \text{institutional substitution model}

M_3 = \text{resource competition model}

M_4 = \text{combined MCH model}

Then compare predictive performance:

P(D \mid M_i)

This prevents the thesis from assuming that MCH is correct simply because it is the proposed theory.

6.11 Out-of-Sample Prediction

Do not only maximize R^2. Ask:

\boxed{\text{Does the model predict unseen societies or periods?}}

Use temporal holdout, geographical holdout, polity holdout, leave-one-region-out, leave-one-period-out. If MCH cannot predict outside its calibration sample, that is important evidence against generality.

6.12 Transition Probability as Primary Outcome

Instead of asking only Is a society cooperative? ask:

P(Y_{t+1} = k \mid Y_t, \mathbf{X}_t, \mathbf{S}_t, \mathbf{I}_t)

This gives a genuine dynamic systems model. For example:

P(C \rightarrow C), \quad P(C \rightarrow \text{Conflict}), \quad P(\text{Conflict} \rightarrow C), \quad P(\text{Conflict} \rightarrow \text{Violence})

6.13 Recovery from Conflict

Study not only C \rightarrow W but also W \rightarrow C. What allows a system to recover?

P(C_{t+1} \mid W_t) = f(\mathbf{I}, T, M, G, \ldots)

This gives the thesis a complete dynamical cycle:

\boxed{
\text{Cooperation} \leftrightarrow \text{Competition} \leftrightarrow \text{Conflict} \leftrightarrow \text{Violence}
}

rather than a one-way deterioration model.

