8. Falsifiability and Null Architecture

8.1 Core Nulls

H1 null:

\beta_{T \times S} = 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \beta_{I \times S} = 0

H2 null:

\frac{\partial P(F)}{\partial \Delta S_I} = 0

H3 null:

\beta_{M \times K_c} = 0

8.2 Evidence Against MCH

The MCH must remain falsifiable. Evidence against it would include:

· Institutional complexity does not systematically increase with social scale.
· Cooperation scales without transformation of its underlying mechanisms.
· Larger social networks do not require additional coordination mechanisms.
· Intergroup conflict is independent of information asymmetry, institutions, or perceived incentives.
· Historical societies systematically contradict the predicted relationship between scale and institutional integration.
· The critical threshold \mathbf{I}^* cannot be estimated independently of the cooperation outcome.
· The distributional prediction (variance of C increases when \mathbf{I} \prec \mathbf{I}^*) fails on out-of-sample data.

The theory must be capable of being rejected.

8.3 Competing Theories

The thesis explicitly competes against alternative explanations. Bayesian model comparison and out-of-sample prediction are used to assess whether MCH outperforms simpler alternatives.

