9. Preliminary Central Thesis

The recurrent emergence of human warfare may not be adequately explained by an intrinsic preference for violence. Instead, warfare may frequently emerge from failures of cooperation across social boundaries, particularly when competition, perceived threat, uncertainty, coalition formation, and institutional limitations interact. Human societies have repeatedly developed cultural and institutional mechanisms capable of scaling cooperation, but these mechanisms may themselves face scaling limits.

The primary phenomenon is scalable cooperation. The failure regime is conflict and violence.

Understanding war therefore requires understanding the architecture and scalability of cooperation.

This proposition remains a research hypothesis, not an established conclusion.

---

10. Conceptual Diagram

The research program can be represented as two coupled pathways.

Cooperation pathway:

\text{Individual cognition} \rightarrow \text{Trust} + \text{Reciprocity} + \text{Reputation} \rightarrow \text{Social Networks} \rightarrow \text{Norms} + \text{Institutions} \rightarrow \text{Large-Scale Cooperation}

Conflict pathway:

\text{Competition} + \text{Threat} + \text{Uncertainty} \rightarrow \text{Coalition Formation} \rightarrow \text{Intergroup Conflict}

The central scientific problem lies at the intersection:

Why does one pathway dominate over the other at a given social scale?

The answer proposed here is institutional scale mismatch \Delta S_I: when the effective scale of social interaction exceeds the capacity of institutions to perform cooperation-preserving functions, the system becomes vulnerable to the conflict pathway. When functional substitution occurs—when mechanisms that no longer scale are replaced by functionally equivalent institutions—cooperation remains dynamically stable.

