11. Expected Contributions

1. Theoretical. A formal multiscale model of cooperation and conflict that explains coexistence, mechanism substitution, functional equivalence, and scale transitions. The concepts of mechanism-specific scaling limits, functional equivalence, institutional scale mismatch, and the scale-transition operator \mathcal{T} provide a new theoretical architecture.
2. Empirical. A systematic Evidence Bank with supporting, contradictory, and boundary-condition evidence; tested scaling laws; and validated predictions on Seshat and archaeological data—with explicit handling of disputed sources and missing data.
3. Methodological. A reproducible protocol for evidence extraction with human verification; a template for coding contested claims; a formal causal graph; Bayesian model comparison; out-of-sample prediction; and pre-registered null models.
4. Practical. Design principles for institutions that remain stable across scales, from communities to international systems. The concept of minimum sufficient governance scale G^* connects the theory to polycentric governance and global commons problems.

The objective is not merely to explain why humans fight, but to identify the structural conditions under which cooperation remains dynamically stable across increasingly large and complex human systems.

