16. The Sharpened MCH

After all revisions, the core hypothesis is:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Multiscale Cooperation Hypothesis:} \\
&\text{Human cooperation is maintained by multiple mechanisms whose} \\
&\text{effectiveness depends on the scale and structure of interaction.} \\
&\text{As social scale increases, mechanisms based on direct knowledge,} \\
&\text{repeated interaction, and interpersonal monitoring may encounter} \\
&\text{informational, demographic, and network constraints.} \\
&\text{Stable cooperation at larger scales therefore requires functional} \\
&\text{substitution, augmentation, or institutionalization of these mechanisms.} \\
&\text{The probability of coordination failure and intergroup conflict should} \\
&\text{increase when the effective scale of social interaction grows faster} \\
&\text{than the capacity of the mechanisms responsible for information,} \\
&\text{coordination, monitoring, legitimacy, enforcement, and dispute resolution.}
\end{aligned}
}

The mathematical core:

\boxed{
\mathbf{X}_{t+1} = \mathcal{T}\left(\mathbf{X}_t, \mathbf{S}_t, \mathbf{I}_t, \mathbf{G}_t, \mathbf{E}_t\right)
}

with:

\boxed{
\Delta S_I = S_{\text{interaction}} - S_{\text{institutional}}
}

and the central prediction:

\boxed{
\Delta S_I > 0 \Rightarrow P(\text{coordination failure}) \uparrow
}

while:

\boxed{
\text{functional substitution} \Rightarrow \Delta S_I \downarrow \Rightarrow P(\text{cooperation}) \uparrow
}

The four-step chain:

\boxed{\text{Cooperation has scaling constraints.}}

\boxed{\text{Different mechanisms fail at different scales.}}

\boxed{\text{Human societies evolve functional substitutes for those mechanisms.}}

\boxed{\text{Conflict becomes more probable when social interaction scales faster than the mechanisms that coordinate it.}}

That four-step chain is the thesis.

Appendices

Appendix A: Mathematical Proofs and Derivations

A.1 Proof of Proposition 1 (Mechanism Scaling)

Proposition 1. Different cooperation mechanisms have different scaling limits. When the interaction scale exceeds the effective operating range of a mechanism, cooperation requires substitution, augmentation, or institutionalization of that mechanism.

Proof sketch. Consider a population of N agents embedded in a network G=(V,E). Let M_j(n) denote the effectiveness of cooperation mechanism j at scale n, defined as the probability that mechanism j sustains cooperation in a randomly selected interaction at scale n.

For direct reciprocity, effectiveness depends on the probability of repeated interaction. Let p_r(n) be the probability that two randomly selected agents interact more than once. In a well-mixed population of size N with interaction probability q per period, p_r(n) \approx q/N. As N \to \infty, p_r(N) \to 0. Hence M_{\text{DR}}(N) \to 0.

For indirect reciprocity, effectiveness depends on information transmission about past behavior. Let I_{\text{trans}}(N) be the probability that information about an agent's past behavior reaches a potential interaction partner. Under realistic constraints, I_{\text{trans}}(N) \to 0 as N \to \infty because the number of possible reputation-relevant interactions grows as O(N^2) while information transmission capacity grows at most as O(N) (Powers & Lehmann, 2017). Hence M_{\text{IR}}(N) \to 0.

For kinship-based cooperation, effectiveness depends on relatedness r(N). In a population of size N, average pairwise relatedness r(N) \approx 1/N under random mating. Hence M_{\text{kin}}(N) \to 0.

For institutional mechanisms, effectiveness depends on monitoring capacity I_m, sanctioning capacity I_s, and information capacity I_i. When these capacities scale appropriately with N, M_{\text{inst}}(N) can remain bounded away from zero. Formally, if I_i(N) \geq c \log N for some c > 0, information can be transmitted sufficiently to support reputation systems; if I_m(N) \geq c' N for some c' > 0, monitoring can cover the population; if I_s(N) \geq c'' N for some c'' > 0, sanctions can be enforced.

Therefore, there exists a scale n_j^* for each non-institutional mechanism j such that M_j(n) < \epsilon for all n > n_j^*, where \epsilon is a threshold below which the mechanism cannot sustain cooperation. When n > n_j^*, cooperation requires either substitution (replacement with a functionally equivalent mechanism) or institutionalization (augmentation of the mechanism with institutional support). \square

A.2 Derivation of the Institutional Scale Mismatch

Define the effective scale of social interaction as:

S_{\text{interaction}} = \sum_{d \in \mathbf{S}} w_d \cdot d

where \mathbf{S} = (P, D_s, H, K, A_n, \tau) is the scale vector and w_d are weights reflecting the relative contribution of each dimension to coordination demands.

Define the effective scale of institutional capacity as:

S_{\text{institutional}} = \sum_{c \in \mathbf{I}} v_c \cdot c

where \mathbf{I} = (I_m, I_i, I_s, I_e, I_l, I_r) is the institutional capacity vector and v_c are weights reflecting the relative contribution of each component to cooperation-preserving functions.

The institutional scale mismatch is:

\Delta S_I = S_{\text{interaction}} - S_{\text{institutional}}

When \Delta S_I > 0, the demands on institutions exceed their capacity. The probability of coordination failure is modeled as:

P(\text{failure}) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp\left(-\lambda \Delta S_I\right)}

where \lambda > 0 is a sensitivity parameter. This yields:

\frac{\partial P(\text{failure})}{\partial \Delta S_I} = \lambda P(\text{failure})(1 - P(\text{failure})) > 0

Thus, increasing institutional scale mismatch monotonically increases the probability of coordination failure.

A.3 Stability Analysis of Cooperative Equilibrium

Let C_t \in [0,1] denote the level of cooperation at time t. The dynamics are:

C_{t+1} = F(C_t) = C_t + \alpha \left[ f(T, R, \mathbf{I}, G, M, Q) - g(R_c, U, B) \right] (1 - C_t) C_t

where \alpha > 0 is an adjustment rate and the bracketed term is the net growth rate of cooperation.

A cooperative equilibrium C^* satisfies F(C^*) = C^*, which implies:

f(T, R, \mathbf{I}, G, M, Q) = g(R_c, U, B)

Local stability requires:

\left| \frac{\partial F}{\partial C} \right|_{C=C^*} < 1

Computing the derivative:

F'(C^*) = 1 + \alpha \left[ f - g \right] (1 - 2C^*)

Since f - g = 0 at equilibrium:

F'(C^*) = 1

This is a marginal case. To assess stability more carefully, consider the second-order Taylor expansion:

F(C) \approx C^* + \frac{1}{2} F''(C^*) (C - C^*)^2

The equilibrium is stable if F''(C^*) < 0:

F''(C^*) = \alpha \left[ f - g \right] (-2) + \alpha \left[ \frac{\partial (f-g)}{\partial C} \right] (1 - 2C^*)

At equilibrium, f - g = 0, so:

F''(C^*) = \alpha \left[ \frac{\partial (f-g)}{\partial C} \right] (1 - 2C^*)

Thus, stability requires:

\frac{\partial (f-g)}{\partial C} (1 - 2C^*) < 0

This condition is satisfied when C^* > 1/2 and \partial (f-g)/\partial C < 0 (diminishing returns to cooperation) or when C^* < 1/2 and \partial (f-g)/\partial C > 0 (increasing returns to cooperation).

A.4 Resilience of Cooperation

Define the basin of attraction of cooperation as:

\mathcal{B}_C = \left\{ C_0 \in [0,1] : \lim_{t \to \infty} C_t = C^* \right\}

The resilience of cooperation is:

\rho_C = \sup \left\{ \delta : C^* - \delta \in \mathcal{B}_C \right\}

That is, \rho_C is the maximum perturbation from the cooperative equilibrium that still returns to C^*.

The MCH predicts that:

\frac{\partial \rho_C}{\partial S_{\text{institutional}}} > 0

and:

\frac{\partial \rho_C}{\partial \Delta S_I} < 0

Higher institutional capacity increases resilience; higher institutional scale mismatch decreases resilience.

A.5 Multinomial Regime Transition Probabilities

The transition probability from regime i to regime j is:

P(Y_{t+1} = j \mid Y_t = i) = \frac{\exp\left(\beta_{ij}^\top \mathbf{X}_t + \gamma_{ij}^\top \mathbf{S}_t + \delta_{ij}^\top (\mathbf{X}_t \times \mathbf{S}_t)\right)}{\sum_{k} \exp\left(\beta_{ik}^\top \mathbf{X}_t + \gamma_{ik}^\top \mathbf{S}_t + \delta_{ik}^\top (\mathbf{X}_t \times \mathbf{S}_t)\right)}

for i, j \in \{C, O, X, W\} where C = cooperation, O = competition, X = nonviolent conflict, W = organized violence.

The MCH predicts the following parameter restrictions:

· \delta_{C \to W, I \times S} < 0: higher institutional capacity reduces the transition from cooperation to violence as scale increases.
· \delta_{C \to W, T \times S} > 0: higher trust has diminishing protective effects as scale increases.
· \delta_{W \to C, I \times S} > 0: institutional capacity facilitates recovery from violence.

