C.1 Causal Graph (DAG)

The causal graph includes the following directed edges:

E \rightarrow R_c \quad \text{(ecological conditions affect resource competition)}

P \rightarrow \mathbf{I} \quad \text{(population size drives institutional demand)}

P \rightarrow U \quad \text{(population size increases anonymity)}

G \rightarrow U \quad \text{(network structure affects information flow)}

U \rightarrow B \quad \text{(uncertainty increases perceived threat)}

\mathbf{I} \rightarrow C \quad \text{(institutions enable cooperation)}

\mathbf{I} \rightarrow W \quad \text{(institutions suppress violence)}

R_c \rightarrow W \quad \text{(resource competition increases violence)}

B \rightarrow W \quad \text{(perceived threat increases violence)}

Feedback loops:

W \rightarrow \mathbf{I} \quad \text{(violence destroys institutional capacity)}

W \rightarrow G \quad \text{(violence disrupts networks)}

This graph identifies:

· Confounders: P, E, G (affect both \mathbf{I} and W)
· Mediators: U, B, R_c
· Colliders: None in the primary path
· Feedback variables: W, \mathbf{I}, G

