Appendix D: Archaeological Baseline Module

D.1 Competing Baselines

Baseline K (Keeley/Kelly). Lethal intergroup violence was common among mobile foragers and ancestral humans. Warfare is ancient, dating to at least the Paleolithic. Expected W: moderate to high.

Baseline F (Fry/Söderberg). Lethal intergroup violence was rare among mobile forager bands. Warfare is not a universal human trait but varies by social and ecological context. Expected W: low.

D.2 Robustness Procedure

For each empirical test, run MCH under both baselines:

\text{MCH}(W^{(K)}) \quad \text{and} \quad \text{MCH}(W^{(F)})

If conclusions survive both, report:

\boxed{\text{robust to archaeological baseline uncertainty}}

If conclusions differ, report the divergence as a substantive finding about the sensitivity of the theory to empirical priors.

D.3 Preservation Bias Quantification

Archaeological evidence for violence is subject to preservation bias. The following variables are coded for each site:

Variable Coding
Skeletal preservation Complete/Partial/Fragmentary
Trauma identification Certain/Probable/Possible
Dating precision High (\pm 50 yrs)/Medium (\pm 200 yrs)/Low (>200 yrs)
