Appendix G: Null Model Architecture

G.1 H1 Null Model

C = \beta_0 + \beta_1 T + \beta_2 \mathbf{I} + \beta_3 S + \beta_4 (T \times S) + \beta_5 (\mathbf{I} \times S) + \epsilon

H1 null: \beta_4 = 0 and \beta_5 = 0.

Alternative: \beta_4 < 0 and \beta_5 > 0.

G.2 H2 Null Model

P(\text{failure}) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp\left(-\lambda \Delta S_I\right)}

H2 null: \lambda = 0 (no effect of institutional mismatch).

Alternative: \lambda > 0.

G.3 H3 Null Model

W = \beta_0 + \beta_1 M + \beta_2 K_c + \beta_3 (M \times K_c) + \epsilon

H3 null: \beta_3 = 0 (no interaction between interdependence and credible commitment).

Alternative: \beta_3 < 0.

G.4 Bayesian Model Comparison

Compare:

M_1 = \text{direct trust model}

M_2 = \text{institutional substitution model}

M_3 = \text{resource competition model}

M_4 = \text{combined MCH model}

Using Bayes factors:

BF_{ij} = \frac{P(D \mid M_i)}{P(D \mid M_j)}

MCH is supported only if M_4 outperforms M_1–M_3 in out-of-sample prediction.

